function pz_what_is_senior_cat_grooming_guide() {
    ob_start(); ?>
    <p>This guide covers how grooming needs shift once a cat reaches their senior years, usually around age 10 and up, and what you can do to fill the gap as self-grooming becomes harder for them. Cats are famously fastidious groomers for most of their lives, so a drop in grooming is often one of the first visible signs that something, often arthritis or dental pain, has changed.</p>
    <p>Beyond just keeping the coat looking nice, senior grooming is really a window into overall health: mats, skin changes, and overgrown nails can all point to pain or mobility issues that deserve a vet's attention, not just a brush.</p>
    <p>The goal here is to help you build a gentle, manageable grooming routine while also knowing which changes are worth flagging to your vet.</p>
    <?php return ob_get_clean();
}

function pz_why_important_senior_cat_grooming_guide() {
    ob_start(); ?>
    <p>A senior cat that stops grooming as thoroughly is not being lazy, it is usually responding to real physical limitations, and recognizing that shift early helps you step in before problems compound.</p>
    <div class="pz-why-grid">
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">🦴</span>
        <div><strong>Arthritis limits a cat's ability to self-groom</strong><p>Arthritis is extremely common in older cats and can make it painful to twist and reach areas like the lower back and hindquarters, which is exactly where mats tend to form first.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">🦷</span>
        <div><strong>Dental pain can reduce grooming along with eating</strong><p>Since cats groom partly with their tongue and mouth, dental disease that causes pain can reduce how often and thoroughly a cat grooms, not just how they eat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">💅</span>
        <div><strong>Claws can overgrow and curl into the paw pad</strong><p>Senior cats often scratch and climb less, which means claws wear down less naturally and can grow long enough to curl back and dig into the pad if not trimmed regularly.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pz-why-item">
        <span class="pz-why-icon">🔍</span>
        <div><strong>Coat and skin changes can signal underlying illness</strong><p>A greasy, matted, or dull coat in a senior cat is sometimes an early visible sign of conditions like hyperthyroidism or kidney disease, making grooming time a useful health check too.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <?php return ob_get_clean();
}

